  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Rounds of showers and thunderstorms impact the \nregion today. The first arrives this morning and the second arrives \nlater in the afternoon. \n\nImpacts from a frontal wave continue through this morning with \nscattered showers and thunderstorms possible. Activity overspreads \nwestern parts of our CWA between 2 and 3 AM before it overspreads \nthe entire region by daybreak. Heavy downpours are likely with rich \nmoisture in place. There is a marginal risk for flash flooding \nacross western MA/CT in the Springfield/Hartford areas, but there is \nno strong signal for anything overly impactful at this time. \nElevated instability will support some rumbles of thunder as showers \nmove across the region, but deep layer shear and low/mid-level lapse \nrates do not appear to be favorable for deep moist convection \ncapable of producing strong to severe storms.\n\nThe passage of the frontal wave brings significant mid-level drying \nwith 700-500 mb RH values falling below 35 percent. The drier \nairmass aloft will result in clearing and strong diurnal heating. \nHigh temperatures will be warmer than they've been over the last \nseveral days with widespread readings in the lower to mid 80s \nlikely. Warmer temps and dew points in the upper 60s to low 70s \nresult in CAPE values climbing to between 1500 and 2000 J/kg around \nthe region. Still, expecting any convection to be isolated to \nscattered in nature with low mid-level RH values. High-Res guidance \nhas generally trended towards this solution with the NAM showing \nisolated convection. The HRRR and RRFS are a bit more aggressive \nand depict clusters of storms developing after 2 pm. Think the \nNBM has a good handle on POPs with widespread values between 35 \nand 45 percent throughout the CWA. Greatest chance for any storm\nactivity will be along and S of a frontal boundary at it makes \nits way SE. Guidance generally shows this boundary S of the Mass\nPike by 18z tomorrow thus, the greatest chance of storms will \nlikely be S of the Mass Pike. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry & very pleasant Fri into early next week \nexcept for some diurnally scattered showers mainly on Sat. Pleasant \ntemps too with highs in the 70s...but lower-middle 80s Fri. \n\nUpper level shortwave energy will close off over the Canadian \nMaritimes over the Holiday Weekend. This will carve out an upper \ntrough over the northeast resulting in pleasant weather. Mainly dry \nweather expected over the majority of this time. However...some \nnorthern stream shortwave energy will probably result in diurnally \ndriven scattered showers mainly on Sat...but can not rule out a few \non Fri. High pressure building southward from eastern Canada Sun \ninto Mon should bring an end to this risk for the latter half of the \nHoliday Weekend. Highs on Fri will mainly be in the lower to middle \n80s...but generally 70s to near 80 this weekend into early next \nweek. In fact...coolest days will be Sat and Sun as the eastern MA \ncoast/Cape and Islands probably only see highs in the upper 60s to \nthe lower 70s with a stiff northeast afternoon breeze. Overnight \nlows mainly in the 50s to the lower 60s...but there might be some \n40s in the normally coolest outlying locations by Sun night/Mon AM \nas high pressure builds across the region. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Elevated risk for dangerous rip currents for the \nHoliday Weekend especially by Sun into Mon. \n\nGiven persistent NE low level flow and swell...we do expect an \nelevated risk for dangerous rip currents for the Holiday Weekend. \nThis is especially true by Sun into Mon on east facing ocean exposed \nbeaches.",
